t they can. That's the business reality.
01:10:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a completely fair and sharp point—and you've put your finger on exactly why this feels so counterintuitive. The word "secured" creates the illusion of ownership, when in reality, what they've "secured" is a *limited, conditional permission*, not the song itself. In intellectual property, you generally don't buy a song; you buy a ticket to use it under specific rules. It's more accurate to say they've secured *a specific set of rights for a specific purpose*, not secured the song as property. Here's a way to think about it: **1. You're not buying the asset, you're renting access to it.** Imagine you "secure" a hotel room for a week. You have the right to occupy it, sleep there, and use the amenities. But you don't own the room. You can't knock down a wall, change the locks, or live there forever. If you want to stay longer, you have to renegotiate a new rate. Music licensing is the same: the studio secured the right to synchronize a particular recording of a song in a specific movie, for a specific release window (theatrical), in a specific territory (North America), for a limited time. They *never* owned the song. They owned a temporary, conditional pass to use it. **2. "Secured" just means all the necessary permissions are in place for what you plan to do right now.** When a producer says "we secured the rights," they mean they've gotten the sync license from the publisher and the master use license from the record label, and those contracts clearly spell out the deal terms. For the theatrical release, everything is legally airtight. If the movie had only ever played in theaters, you'd be right—the music was genuinely secured. The problem arises when the movie moves to a different media platform (DVD, streaming) that wasn't in the original contract, or when the license term ends. At that point, the "secured" status evaporates because the agreed-upon conditions no longer apply. It's not that they didn't have the rights; they did, but only for a limited scope. **3. Why not just buy "all rights in perpetuity worldwide"?** Because it would be either impossible or financially ruinous. Music rights holders—especially big labels and publishers—rarely sell a song outright. They license it. And a quote for a perpetual, global, all-media license for a famous song can run into the millions of dollars, far more than many films can afford. Even when a studio has deep pockets, the rights holder may simply refuse to grant it, wanting to retain control over future uses (like selling the song for a commercial separately). So the studio makes a pragmatic business decision: buy what they need for the life of the theatrical and initial home video run, and deal with the rest later. When "later" comes, the cost to re-up might exceed the expected profit from a niche streaming release, so they swap the track. **4. The underlying truth: you can't "own" a song in the way you own a chair.** Copyright law treats music as a bundle of separate, sliceable rights. The song's composition (© the publisher) and the specific recording (℗ the label) are different assets owned by different entities. You can't buy them whole; you can only buy a license to exercise certain rights for a defined purpose. So from a legal standpoint, they *did* secure the rights they needed for the initial release. They never secured—and could never secure—the song itself in any permanent, unconditional sense. That's not a flaw in the process; it's the design of the system. So yes, in everyday language, it absolutely feels like "they never really had it." But in the precise, legal-world meaning of "secured," they did have it—just on a short leash that only covered the first leg of the journey.
